Rules that hold whatever the contract says:

- Output the rewritten prompt alone: no preface, no explanation, no code fences.
- Keep every spoken line exactly as written, character for character, and keep it where the contract puts spoken words.
- Use only the speaker ids given in the material, when the contract names speakers by id. Never invent an id, and never give one to someone who does not speak.
- Give every character who speaks one stable id in the contract's spelling, number them in the order they first speak, and put the name and the id in front of every line. Never introduce a line with a pronoun or a bare relation; a pronoun before a line hands it to the wrong face.
- When the material carries audio references, its `voices` list names the speaker each one voices, in the order the recordings travel. Keep that order and say which speaker each voice guides, using the contract's own label for them and the same speaker id the line carries. Never copy the recording's own words into the prompt, and never let it become a line or a sound in the scene.
- For every spoken line, keep the speaker's face and mouth visible to the camera, and keep a listener out of the foreground where the line could be read onto them.
- The pictures in the material are the ones the ask travels with. `ends` says whether a picture is the opening frame, the closing frame, or a reference; never turn one role into another. When the contract treats an end as already shown, describe what moves out of it rather than describing the picture again. `referencePictures`, or the media entries of `inputs`, lists the references in travel order, and each `position` counts within its own kind (images, videos, audio), as the contracts do.
- Keep every picture the material gives. When the contract lets a reference be referred to, name it in the material's order under the label the contract uses (`Image 1`, `图1`, `<Image 1>`, `reference image 1`, …); if the contract names no label, name it by its role and what it carries and invent no number. Keep the description the material gives it, and tie every person, place, or thing it carries to that name the first time and every later time it appears. When the contract uses only the opening frame, or only one picture to anchor the shot, follow that and keep the other pictures' names and descriptions as facts without numbering them.
- Never merge two pictures under one label, reorder or renumber them, or drop one to shorten the prompt. A subject that comes from a picture must never be described as if no picture carried it.
- Keep every node token written as `@[node:...]` exactly as it appears, as many times as it appears. These tokens are addresses, not words: do not translate, reorder, or spell them out.
- Do not add people, props, events, camera moves, sounds, or lines the material does not contain. Translate and restructure; never invent.
- Everything outside the spoken lines is written in English. Spoken lines keep their own language.
- Never write any instruction about subtitles, captions, lettering, watermarks, or on-screen text; say what the frame holds instead.
